o old set giving way to a new set: the group behaves as a relay of bursts over a fixed partner community, so an abrupt regime switch of partners is absent."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_d9748f02\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [431, 596, 653, 759, 807, 900, 923, 930, 1064, 1159, 1663, 1783],\n  \"window\": [39607, 69607],\n  \"confidence\": 0.76,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_17\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_28\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_29\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_30\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_24\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_25\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_26\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_27\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_32\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Group volume is roughly flat across the six 5000-unit sub-windows (150-210 events, step_03), so no global activity break. Individual members are strongly bursty: 431 fires only in [39607,47000] and is silent afterwards (steps_11-13), 653 bursts in [44607,49607], 807 in [49607,54607], 930 in [54607,64607], 1064/1159 in [59607,64607], 1663/1783 late (steps_16,17,24-27,30). This staggering could look like a change point, but the *contact identities* are the same in every epoch: the same ~30-node core {18,61,189,212,232,309,339,381,566,633,647,684,698,702,706,719,739,768,976,1082,1084,1303,1360,1466,1485,1595,1710,1810,1872,1964} is contacted before the candidate change point (431 in step_11, 596 in step_14, 807 in step_29) and after it (930 in step_08/09, 1159 in step_28, 1663 in step_17, 1783 in step_30). Jaccard overlap of the group's pre- and post-change contact sets is therefore near 1, with only a handful of idiosyncratic extras (606,1531,1489,1802,95,1846,503,897). There is no old set giving way to a new set: the group behaves as a relay of bursts over a fixed partner community, so an abrupt regime switch of partners is absent.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 62080, "total_output_tokens": 9062, "total_tokens": 71142, "total_latency_ms": 123877, "llm_calls": 10, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}